  "full_text": "So, why don't we just declare all military personnel in Arizona and Nevada and research scientists in the defense industry. They are now declared citizens of the Republic. I don't know what we want to call it. And we should take a vote on maybe a 200-person Senate. So, now, extreme elitism is kind of disgusting and it's unhealthy. On the other hand, I've learned something, which is it's not a virtue if you're weak and you have a problem saying no. We don't want women and we don't really want to be run by niggers, okay? So, it's... It's weakness. You have to learn to say no, you know. Extreme elitism is kind of ugly and unhealthy, but on the other hand, being weak is just, that's not a virtue, okay. Don't pat yourself on the back because you think you're so loving and stuff. It's weakness, okay. You have a problem, you can't say no, okay. So we probably want to set up a path to citizenship for the people in the residence. Maybe they can do that through the military. Basically, it's voting rights. I don't really know what the... I know that Rome had two types of people, citizens and non-citizens. Maybe they had slaves. I don't know what they had. But anyway, the citizens were special, and it was a cherished honor. And basically, they had voting rights. So anyway, I don't... So...",
